# The queue is like the "stack" of a typical programing language. Rules will add
# statements to the queue which will be executed in order, but which may also
# call "functions" so-to-speak that replace the statement with a list of other
# statements at it's location in the queue. This lets us emulate something like
# a call stack to reliably execute things in order, where reform doesn't
# natively have any direct mechanism for executing a series of steps in a line.

# Create the empty queue.
$ queue

# The queue will have "statements" added to it like:
# 
# queue
#   stmt-start say (hello world) stmt-end
#   stmt-start say (goodbye world) stmt-end
#   stmt-start now hat is green stmt-end
# 
# Rules that operate on the `say` statement will look for a fact starting with
# `queue stmt-start say` and it will know at that point that it should start
# processing the statement.
# 
# The statement should remove the queue fact and create a new queue, which may
# have either modified the say statement, removed it so the next one can process
# or even added new ones to the beginning of the queue as a prt of it's "call
# stack".

# These three rules split sentences into "statements" which are split
# up by periods and had trailing periods removed. This lets the rest
# of the parser operate on statements in the queue instead of sentences directly
# and it means we can automatically support having multiple statements on one
# line, separated by periods.
$ rule (split sentences by periods 1)
    (
        - parse $( $words1 )+ . $( $words2 )+
        - queue $( $queue )*
    )
    (
        queue stmt-start $( $words1 )+ stmt-end $( $queue )*
        parse $( $words2 )+
    )
$ rule (split sentences by periods 2)
    (
        - parse $( $words )+ $( . )?
        - queue $( $queue )*
    )
    (
        queue stmt-start $( $words )+ stmt-end $( $queue )*
    )
    

# This rule will print a warning if any statement was not understood.
$ rule (warn about statements not understood)
    (
        - queue stmt-start $( $arg )* stmt-end $( $queue )*
    )
    (
        $ println (Warning: I did not understand this statement:

    ) ($($arg )*) (
)
        queue $( $queue )*
    )